    faqs: [
      {
        question: "Is foam rolling the IT band actually useful?",
        answer: "The premise is dated. Fairclough and colleagues' anatomical work (Journal of Anatomy 2006, Journal of Science and Medicine in Sport 2007) showed the IT band is a thickened part of the sheet of tissue that wraps the thigh (the fascia lata), firmly attached along the thigh bone. It is not a rope that slides back and forth over the bony bump at the knee (the epicondyle), so the classic 'friction' explanation does not hold. Rolling can feel good and may briefly reduce sensitivity, but it does not meaningfully lengthen the band. Strengthening the hip and adjusting how much you run is where the real change happens."
      },
      {
        question: "Why does my ITB hurt at a predictable distance into a run?",
        answer: "It is a problem of load over time. The muscles on the side of the hip (abductors) tire over a long run, control of the pelvis drifts, and the tissue on the outside of the knee under the IT band gets squeezed more and more. Pain often shows up at a consistent distance because that is when capacity runs out. Noehren and colleagues (JOSPT 2014) found the picture is not always simple hip weakness when strength is tested without movement, but how well the muscles resist tiring and how you run still matter clinically."
      },
      {
        question: "Should I stop running entirely?",
        answer: "Not usually. Complete rest often feels good short-term, then symptoms return within the first few runs because nothing has changed in what the knee can handle or how you run. The usual approach is running less, avoiding downhill running and steeply sloped roads (cambers) early on, and pairing adjusted running with a structured hip and trunk strengthening program. Many runners can keep running at reduced load while rehab progresses."
      },
      {
        question: "How long does ITB syndrome take to resolve?",
        answer: "Some runners improve over several weeks when hip strengthening, load management, and running-form work are addressed together, while others need longer. Getting back to running and getting back to full training are separate milestones, both guided by symptoms and tolerance. Rest alone often does not settle it for long, and symptoms that have been present for a long time can take longer to settle."
      },
      {
        question: "Does cadence actually help IT band pain?",
        answer: "Sometimes. Taking slightly quicker steps, around 5 to 10 percent above your usual step rate (cadence), tends to shorten your stride, reduce how much you bounce up and down, and lower the peak load on the outside of the knee. It is a useful add-on rather than a fix on its own. Mucha and colleagues' 2017 review of risk factors in runners and Louw's 2014 biomechanical review both point to running mechanics as one of several contributors worth addressing."
      },
      {
        question: "Does ITB syndrome need imaging or injections?",
        answer: "Usually not. The diagnosis is made from your history and an examination: pain on the outside of the knee brought on by single-leg tasks with weight on the leg or by longer runs, tenderness near the bony bump on the outside of the knee, and a recognisable pattern in your training history. Imaging is considered when the story does not fit, when pain is not settling with a well-built program, or when a meniscus problem or a problem in the outer half of the knee joint is suspected. Injections are not a first-choice treatment."
      },
      {
        question: "Can strength work alone fix ITB syndrome?",
        answer: "Often, when combined with load management. The hip muscles that move the leg out to the side, turn it outward and extend it usually need work, and so do how much you run and how much of it is on hills. Patients who do strengthening without adjusting training tend to stall, and those who adjust training without strengthening tend to relapse."
      },
      {
        question: "Why is downhill running so much worse?",
        answer: "Downhill running increases knee bending in the 20 to 30 degree range, where the tissues under the IT band are squeezed most against the bony bump on the outside of the knee. It also asks more of the hip muscles, which have to control the movement while lengthening (eccentric control). That combination is why steep descents often flare symptoms. Early rehab usually avoids hard downhill work and reintroduces it gradually once strength and running form are holding up."
      }
    ],
