        question: "Do I need an MRI for a sprained ankle?",
        answer: "Usually no. Most outer ankle sprains are diagnosed from the examination and do not require MRI. I flag it to your family doctor or specialist and refer you for imaging when symptoms are not improving as expected at 6 to 8 weeks, when there is suspicion of a high ankle (syndesmotic) injury, damage to the cartilage and bone surface inside the ankle joint (an osteochondral lesion of the talus), a peroneal tendon tear, or when mechanical symptoms like locking or catching persist. X-rays per Ottawa Ankle Rules are the appropriate first imaging step if indicated acutely."
