    faqs: [
      {
        question: "Do I need an X-ray before starting physiotherapy for hip osteoarthritis?",
        answer: "Usually no. The 2025 JOSPT Hip OA Clinical Practice Guideline and OARSI 2019 both describe hip OA as diagnosed from age, symptom pattern and examination findings. An X-ray can confirm the picture when it is unclear or when surgery is being considered, but how bad the X-ray looks matches pain and function poorly. I have seen patients with minimal changes on imaging who are in significant pain, and others with severe changes who walk for an hour without symptoms."
      },
      {
        question: "Will exercise make my hip arthritis worse?",
        answer: "Not when it is dosed sensibly. The Cochrane review of exercise for hip OA (Fransen et al., 2014) found small-to-moderate reductions in pain and improvements in function, with no sign of harm. The 2025 JOSPT clinical practice guideline strongly recommends individualised strengthening, flexibility and endurance programmes. Flares tend to follow a sudden jump beyond what the hip currently tolerates, rather than loading itself, so getting the dose right matters more than avoidance."
      },
      {
        question: "Can physiotherapy help me avoid a hip replacement?",
        answer: "For many people, yes, at least for years. Strong exercise evidence backs meaningful improvement in symptoms and function. That said, I will not oversell it. The Bennell JAMA 2014 trial specifically found that a structured physiotherapy programme combining several treatments did not do better than a placebo (sham) treatment over 36 weeks, which has been debated widely since. My read of the broader literature, including the 2025 JOSPT revision, is that consistent long-term loading, weight management and education can meaningfully change the course for many patients, and surgery remains a reasonable option when non-surgical care has stopped making progress and pain is dominating daily life."
      },
      {
        question: "Why does my hip hurt in the groin rather than the side?",
        answer: "The hip joint sits deeper and further forward than most people expect, so pain from the joint is often felt in the groin or front of the thigh and can travel to the knee. Several tissues in and around the joint can contribute, so where the pain sits is only one part of the assessment. Pain over the outer hip bone more often points to the buttock tendons (gluteal tendons) than to the joint itself. This is why I examine hip rotation carefully: losing the ability to turn the hip inward (internal rotation) is one of the more useful examination findings in hip OA."
      },
      {
        question: "Is it safe to keep running with hip osteoarthritis?",
        answer: "In many cases, yes, with adjustments. Running is not the cause of hip OA in most people. Total distance, terrain, recovery and overall hip strength matter more than the act of running itself. I usually keep runners with symptoms running at a reduced dose guided by symptoms, while they build strength in the muscles that move the leg out to the side and back (abductors and extensors). Then they rebuild distance gradually, guided by how the hip feels over the following 24 hours. Trail running and downhill running are the two I temporarily reduce first."
      },
      {
        question: "What about glucosamine, collagen, and supplements?",
        answer: "OARSI 2019 issued conditional recommendations against glucosamine and chondroitin for hip OA, citing inconsistent evidence on pain and function. Collagen and turmeric data are mixed. I do not discourage patients from trying low-risk options, but I do not want supplements displacing the treatments with the strongest evidence: exercise, education, and weight management where relevant. A physician or pharmacist can discuss whether an oral anti-inflammatory medicine is suitable for flare days, taking your other conditions and medicines into account."
      },
      {
        question: "How long until I feel a real difference?",
        answer: "Most patients notice meaningful change in morning stiffness and how far they can walk within 4 to 6 weeks of consistent loading. Strength gains and bigger improvements in daily function come closer to the 3-month mark. The 2025 JOSPT clinical practice guideline and OARSI 2019 both describe hip OA management as an ongoing project rather than a short course of treatment. My goal is to get you to a programme you will actually keep doing twice a week for the long term."
      },
      {
        question: "Does cracking or grinding in the hip mean it is getting worse?",
        answer: "No. This grinding (crepitus) is common and does not match joint damage or outcomes well. A quiet hip is not necessarily a healthier hip. If clicking comes with sharp pain, giving way, or the hip truly locking, that changes the conversation, and I examine for an injury to the labrum (the rim of cartilage around the socket). Painless noise on its own is not a reason to stop exercise."
      }
    ],

