    faqs: [
      {
        question: "If I have cam or pincer morphology on imaging, do I definitely have FAI?",
        answer: "No. The Warwick Agreement is explicit that FAI is a syndrome that needs symptoms, plus findings on examination, plus findings on imaging. A large portion of adults without symptoms have a cam or pincer bone shape and live without hip pain. Shape alone does not diagnose this. What matters is whether the hip is painful every time it is put in the pinch position, and whether that matches your day-to-day complaints."
      },
      {
        question: "Should I just have hip arthroscopy?",
        answer: "Sometimes that is the right call, but not as a first step. The UK FASHIoN trial (Griffin et al., Lancet 2018) favoured keyhole surgery (arthroscopy) over personalised hip therapy at 12 months. Mansell et al. (AJSM 2018) found no significant difference between arthroscopy and physical therapy at 2 years, although many people switched between groups. The trials tested specific rehabilitation programs and reached different results, so neither settles the question for every person. My approach, consistent with the Warwick Agreement, is about 3 to 6 months of genuinely progressed loading and movement retraining first, and surgery considered only if that fails or the patient's life demands do not allow waiting."
      },
      {
        question: "Why does getting out of a low car hurt so much?",
        answer: "A low car seat plus turning to get out bends your hip deeply, brings the knee across your body and turns the hip inward, all at the same time. That is the exact combination that a cam-type bone shape does not tolerate well. Raising the seat, sliding one leg out first before turning, and standing up before turning often makes a big difference until strength catches up."
      },
      {
        question: "Should I stop squatting and deadlifting?",
        answer: "Usually not stop, just change the range. Squats to a box that stops your hip bending as far as the painful pinch are often well tolerated. Romanian deadlifts, which focus on hinging at the hips and keep the knee fairly straight, tend to be well tolerated. I treat lifting as part of the rehab rather than something to avoid."
      },
      {
        question: "Are hip flexor stretches safe for FAI?",
        answer: "Hard hip flexor stretches taken to the limit, such as the couch stretch or a deep lunge, push the front of the hip to its limit and often flare an irritable FAI hip, even though the pinch itself happens when the hip bends and turns. I usually swap them for controlled strength work that moves the leg backward (hip extension), which works the front of the hip through a useful range and is less likely to flare it. If stretching is helping the other side or the lower back, keep it there, just not aggressively into the painful hip."
      },
      {
        question: "Can I still do yoga or pilates?",
        answer: "Often yes, with edits. Deep or forced hip positions, including some versions of pigeon or lotus, can aggravate an irritable hip. Changing the depth, adding support, and not forcing a painful end range usually let people keep practising. I coach patients to own the modifications rather than stop the practice."
      },
      {
        question: "How long before I know if conservative care is going to work?",
        answer: "Most patients with FAI syndrome notice a meaningful change in day-to-day catching within 4 to 6 weeks of proper load management and targeted strengthening. Full strength gains and confident return to sport typically take 3 to 6 months. If after about 3 to 6 months I still cannot progress your loading without flaring the hip, that is when a surgical opinion becomes relevant."
      }
    ],

