    faqs: [
      {
        question: "Why does my outer hip hurt at night?",
        answer: "Night pain is one of the most typical features of gluteal tendinopathy, and it is often linked to the tendon being squeezed against the bony point of the hip (the greater trochanter) for long periods. Lying on the painful side presses directly on the tendon. Lying on the other side with the top knee falling forward stretches the IT band across the tendon. Both positions squeeze the tendon for hours, at exactly the time the tissue needs to recover. Changing how you sleep is often the fastest way to reduce overall symptoms."
      },
      {
        question: "Can I sleep on my side with GTPS?",
        answer: "Yes, with some setup. Start on the non-painful side with a firm pillow between the knees and a second pillow between the ankles, keeping the top leg level with the pelvis rather than falling across the body. Some patients find a small pillow tucked behind them more comfortable than sleeping fully on the back. The goal is to stop the thigh crossing towards the other leg (adduction), the position that squeezes the gluteal tendons."
      },
      {
        question: "Why is walking uphill worse for my hip?",
        answer: "Walking uphill, climbing stairs and stepping onto a curb all ask more of the muscles on the side of the hip (abductors), which keep the pelvis level while you stand on one leg. In gluteal tendinopathy the tendon can be less tolerant of load for a while, so these tasks can push it past what it currently handles. Bergmann's study of four people with hip implants that measured force (Journal of Biomechanics, 2001) recorded roughly 238% of body weight through the hip joint during normal walking, and more on stairs. That is joint load rather than tendon load, but it shows how hard the hip muscles work. This is part of why building up exposure gradually, rather than avoiding these tasks, is the rehabilitation approach."
      },
      {
        question: "Are cortisone injections good for GTPS?",
        answer: "The LEAP trial (Mellor et al., BMJ 2018) compared education plus exercise with a single corticosteroid injection and with a wait-and-see approach. Education plus exercise gave better overall improvement than the injection at 8 weeks and at 52 weeks, and less pain at 8 weeks; by 52 weeks pain levels were similar. An injection can reduce pain in the short term, but it does not rebuild the tendon. I reserve injections for cases where pain is preventing someone from engaging with loading rehabilitation at all."
      },
      {
        question: "How long does gluteal tendinopathy take to heal?",
        answer: "Most patients see symptoms start to ease within 4 to 6 weeks of starting load management and isometric exercises (holds that do not move the joint). Building real tendon capacity typically takes 3 to 6 months, sometimes longer when symptoms have been present for over a year. Women after menopause may take longer because hormones affect tendon tissue. Sleeping through the night and walking uphill without an ache are the two markers I lean on to confirm the tendon has rebuilt meaningful capacity."
      },
      {
        question: "Should I stretch my IT band if I have lateral hip pain?",
        answer: "Generally no. Classic IT band stretches, like crossing one leg behind the other and leaning sideways, pull the thigh across towards the other leg (adduction), the exact position that squeezes the gluteal tendon against the bony point of the hip. Foam rolling directly over the outer hip does the same. Well-intentioned, but it often keeps the pain flared. Load management, strengthening that does not squeeze the tendon, and changes to posture are more productive."
      },
      {
        question: "Is walking safe with lateral hip pain?",
        answer: "Usually, in some form. Complete rest is rarely helpful, and losing fitness makes the problem worse. What helps is reducing what sets it off: avoid walking to the point of a flare, break up long walks, choose flatter routes in early rehab, and hold off on hill repeats until tolerance builds. I usually prescribe a specific daily walking dose and adjust it weekly based on how your symptoms respond."
      },
      {
        question: "Is GTPS more common in women?",
        answer: "Yes. Population research by Segal et al. (2007) reported an odds ratio of roughly 3.4 for women compared with men in adults aged 50 to 79. It is commonly seen in middle age and around menopause. Possible contributors include a wider pelvis, which increases the squeezing force through the IT band, and hormonal shifts that affect tendon collagen. The treatment approach is the same, but it does shape the conversation about timeline and expectations."
      }
    ],

