    faqs: [
      {
        question: "Is my hip bursitis actually bursitis?",
        answer: "Usually not as a problem on its own. The term stuck around for decades, but imaging and surgical studies showed that most outer hip pain labelled trochanteric bursitis is really a problem of the buttock tendons (gluteal tendinopathy), sometimes with the bursa irritated as well. The practical point: treatment that targets the tendon (progressive loading, reducing compression) works. Treatment that targets only the bursa (rest, repeated steroid injections) usually does not hold."
      },
      {
        question: "Will a cortisone injection fix it?",
        answer: "It can ease pain in the short term, but it does not rebuild the tendon. The LEAP trial (Mellor et al., BMJ 2018) compared education and exercise against corticosteroid injection and a wait-and-see approach. At 8 weeks, steroid injection beat waiting. Education plus exercise did better than injection on overall improvement at 8 weeks and at 12 months, though by 12 months pain levels were similar. Injection is worth considering selectively when pain is stopping someone from engaging with rehab, not as the plan itself. Injection is not something I do myself. When that route makes sense, I coordinate with a sports medicine physician who handles the procedure, and I plan the rehab work around it."
      },
      {
        question: "Why does it hurt most at night?",
        answer: "Because lying on the sore side squeezes the tendon and bursa against the bony point of the hip (the greater trochanter) for hours, and lying on the other side lets the top leg drop across the body (adduction), which also squeezes them. A pillow between the knees, a softer mattress topper on the sore side, or sleeping partly on your back usually changes things within a week or two."
      },
      {
        question: "Should I stretch my IT band or foam roll it?",
        answer: "No, not while it is irritable. Hard IT band stretching and foam rolling directly over the bony point of the hip squeeze the exact structures that are already being squeezed. I swap those for gentle mobility work through the hip and trunk, and targeted loading of the buttock muscles (glutes)."
      },
      {
        question: "How long does this take to settle?",
        answer: "A typical time frame is 6 to 12 weeks for meaningful symptom change and 3 to 6 months to build durable capacity. Longer in people who have had it for years or who keep returning to the sleeping and sitting positions that loaded it. The LEAP programme ran 14 sessions over 8 weeks, which is a reasonable yardstick for the active phase."
      },
      {
        question: "Can I still run or walk?",
        answer: "Usually yes, at a reduced and adjusted dose. Flat walking is generally fine. Running often stays in for recreational runners if there is no overnight flare. Hill running, long runs and running on sloped roads (camber) are the usual early triggers. I set the dose by how it feels the next morning rather than by pushing through."
      },
      {
        question: "Do I need an MRI or ultrasound?",
        answer: "Not routinely. Imaging finds tendon changes and fluid in the bursa in many people without outer hip pain, so the result rarely changes the plan. I cannot order imaging myself, so I flag it to your family doctor or a sports medicine physician and refer you when imaging would change the plan: when the history is unusual, when symptoms do not progress after a fair trial of loading, or when an injection guided by imaging is being considered."
      }
    ],

