    faqs: [
      {
        question: "How long am I out?",
        answer: "It depends on grade and location. A typical grade 1 returns to sport in 2 to 3 weeks. Grade 2 usually takes 4 to 8 weeks. Grade 3 complete tears sit at 3 to 6 months. The British Athletics Muscle Injury Classification (Pollock et al., BJSM 2014) grades where the injury sits, and a later study in elite track and field athletes (Pollock et al., BJSM 2016) found that injuries extending into the tendon (type c) took longer to return from and came back more often, so the MRI location can change the timeline. Time frames are useful for planning, but return is determined by meeting criteria, not by the calendar."
      },
      {
        question: "Sprinting or stretching injury, and does it matter?",
        answer: "It matters a lot. Askling described two patterns that behave very differently. Sprinting-type strains happen at high speed and usually heal faster. Stretching-type strains happen when the muscle is stretched long (a high kick, a hurdler's stretch, water skiing) and take significantly longer to settle. In the Askling 2013 trial, stretching-type injuries took nearly twice as long to return as sprinting-type on the same protocol. The history decides how hard I push loading at the end of the muscle's range."
      },
      {
        question: "Why do hamstring injuries come back?",
        answer: "Two main reasons. First, returning to full pace before the tissue has rebuilt its capacity when the muscle is stretched long. Second, dropping the lengthening (eccentric) work once the early pain has gone. Most recurrences happen at or near the original site, which suggests the scarred area stays more vulnerable to strain if the surrounding tissue is not loaded properly."
      },
      {
        question: "Do I have to do Nordic hamstring curls?",
        answer: "Not necessarily, but they can be useful, particularly in sports with sprinting or kicking. Van Dyk et al. (BJSM 2019) pooled 15 studies across 8,459 athletes and found that prevention programmes including the Nordic roughly halved hamstring injury rates; that is a prevention effect in athletes, not a treatment effect for an existing tear. Nordics need a tolerable starting level and are one part of a program that also rebuilds running and sport-specific capacity, and other exercises may be used when they better fit you or your stage of recovery."
      },
      {
        question: "When can I start running again?",
        answer: "When single-leg hops, hamstring strength tested without movement (isometric strength), and pain-free lengthening (eccentric) exercises are equal on both sides. Running usually returns in phase two for most grade 1 and low-grade 2 strains. Start on flat ground at an easy pace for short durations, and build distance before speed. Speed work waits until late phase three."
      },
      {
        question: "Is stretching a good idea?",
        answer: "In the early, painful phase, hard stretching to the limit usually delays healing, particularly for stretching-type injuries. Gentle range-of-movement work and early lengthening (eccentric) loading do more for long-term length than holding stretches. I rarely prescribe formal hamstring stretches in the early weeks."
      },
      {
        question: "Do I need an MRI?",
        answer: "Not for most straightforward strains. I cannot order imaging myself, so I flag it to your family doctor or a sports medicine physician and refer you when it would change the plan: a suspected tendon pulled off the bone at the top (a proximal avulsion, urgently), an in-season injury in a high-level athlete where the timeline drives selection, or recovery that is stalling. The JOSPT 2022 clinical practice guideline considers imaging useful for predicting recovery in competitive athletes but not necessary for most rehab plans."
      },
      {
        question: "What if I feel it twinge again while running?",
        answer: "Stop the session that day and drop back one phase in the loading plan for 3 to 5 days. A twinge does not mean the rehab has failed. Most often it means the next step in the progression was too big or the warm-up was too short. I reassess strength without movement (isometric testing), pain-free range and whether hops are equal on both legs before resuming at the amount you last tolerated."
      }
    ],

