    faqs: [
      {
        question: "Why does the outside of my ankle hurt if I did not roll it recently?",
        answer: "A history of rolled ankles years ago still matters. Once the ligaments on the outside of the ankle have been stretched, the peroneal tendons take on more of the stabilising work during walking and running, particularly on uneven ground. Over time that extra demand can overload the tendons even without a fresh sprain. This is why peroneal tendinopathy and chronic ankle instability so often travel together, and why rehab has to address both the tendon and the underlying instability rather than only the painful side."
      },
      {
        question: "How long does peroneal tendinopathy usually take to settle?",
        answer: "Improvement often takes weeks to months, and a full return to running, hiking, or cutting sports can take a few months. Cases that have been grumbling for over a year, or that have developed on a background of untreated ankle instability, often take longer. Research cannot reliably rank the main predictors for one person; ankle stability, activity demands, and how the tendon responds to loading help guide the plan."
      },
      {
        question: "Should I rest completely, or keep moving?",
        answer: "Complete rest is rarely the answer. The tendon needs load to remodel, and long periods off just delay the conversation. Many people do better on a modified plan: reduce the aggravating volume, swap uneven-terrain running for flatter surfaces for a few weeks, and add a specific peroneal loading program twice weekly. One way to judge the dose: pain during activity stays moderate, settles within 24 hours, and does not build from week to week. These are example monitoring limits, not proof that running is safe for every cause of pain, so the plan is adjusted to how your tendon responds."
      },
      {
        question: "Do orthotics help peroneal tendinopathy?",
        answer: "They can, particularly when the foot habitually sits tilted onto its outer edge (chronic inversion), or when a long-standing high-arched (cavus) foot is overloading the outer side of the foot. A wedge or flare along the outer edge of the orthotic takes load off the peroneal tendons while you stand and walk. Orthotics are a support, not a cure. I use them as a bridge while strengthening rebuilds capacity, not as a long-term substitute for the loading program."
      },
      {
        question: "What is the difference between peroneal tendinopathy and an ankle sprain that just will not heal?",
        answer: "A typical outer ankle sprain irritates the ligament at the front of the joint (the anterior talofibular ligament), and symptoms usually centre on a specific spot just below the bony bump on the outside. Peroneal tendinopathy sits behind and below that bump, travels down the outer foot, and is reliably brought on by turning the foot outward against resistance. Sprains that do not settle after 6 to 8 weeks often have a peroneal component that has been missed, and the rehab needs to shift toward tendon loading rather than the exercises used early after a sprain."
      },
      {
        question: "Can I keep running with peroneal tendinopathy?",
        answer: "Usually yes, with adjustments. Dropping weekly mileage by around a third, running on flatter surfaces temporarily, and spacing runs at least 48 hours apart gives the tendon time to adapt. Hills, trails, and speed work come back last. If pain keeps climbing during runs, or morning stiffness is lasting noticeably longer than usual, the loading has probably outpaced the tendon and the plan needs to scale back for a week or two."
      },
      {
        question: "Is an ankle brace a good idea, or does it make me weaker?",
        answer: "In the short term, a lace-up brace during higher-risk activity, such as trail runs, basketball, or hiking, reduces the twisting force that rolls the ankle inward and strains the tendon and often allows training to continue. It does not weaken the foot provided rehab keeps building strength underneath it. The brace is a partner to the loading program, not a replacement. Most people phase it out once tolerance is back and balance work is consistent."
      }
    ],

